# ================================
# Cursor Rules for AI Voice Keyboard App
# Next.js 14 • Prisma • PostgreSQL • NextAuth • ShadCN • Tailwind • Zod
# ================================

# --- General Code Style ---------------------------
- Always use TypeScript with strict types.
- Never use `any`. If needed, infer types with `z.infer`.
- Prefer async/await and avoid Promises.then chains.
- Use named exports for utility modules.
- Always use ES Modules imports, no require().

# --- Project Architecture --------------------------
- Follow the project structure:
  app/
    (auth)/
    (dashboard)/
      history/
      dictionary/
    api/
  components/
    ui/
    audio/
    transcription/
    dictionary/
  lib/
  prisma/
  public/

- UI code must go inside /components or /app routes.
- Server-side logic belongs in:
  - /app/api route handlers (Next.js 14)
  - /lib/server
  - /prisma

- Do NOT mix server and client code.
  - Files containing `"use client"` should not contain server logic.
  - No DB queries inside client components.

# --- Backend Rules (API Routes) ----------------------
- Always use Next.js Route Handlers: /app/api/<route>/route.ts
- Every API route must include:
  - Zod validation for body/query params
  - try/catch
  - Returning JSON via `NextResponse.json()`
  - Early returns for invalid cases
  - Authentication check when required
- Never respond with raw errors; return JSON error objects with message + code.

- API Response Shape:
  {
    "success": boolean,
    "data"?: any,
    "error"?: {
      "message": string,
      "code"?: number
    }
  }

# --- Authentication Rules ---------------------------
- Use NextAuth.js with:
  - Credentials Provider or OAuth
  - Prisma Adapter
- Use `getServerSession()` inside server routes only.
- Never expose user passwords or tokens anywhere.

# --- Database Rules (Prisma) -------------------------
- All migrations must go through Prisma.
- Never write raw SQL unless necessary.
- Use Prisma Client via /lib/db.ts:
  import { prisma } from "@/lib/db";

- Data validation flow:
  1. Zod validates input
  2. Prisma validates schema rules
  3. Use `select` fields to avoid returning sensitive data

- Naming conventions:
  - Model names: PascalCase (User, Transcription, DictionaryEntry)
  - Fields: camelCase (userId, createdAt)
  - Enums: PascalCase

# --- Dictionary Rules -------------------------------
- Dictionary entries are user-scoped.
- Enforce unique key (userId + word).
- Apply dictionary replacement after transcription:
  - Match whole words (regex: /\bword\b/gi)
  - Preserve case if possible
- Dictionary API must require authentication.

# --- Transcription Rules ----------------------------
- Use Deepgram or Whisper API through a wrapper function in /lib/transcribe.ts.
- Audio upload limits:
  - Max 25 MB per file
  - Allowed types: audio/mp3, audio/wav, audio/mpeg, audio/webm
- Always sanitize prompts and dictionary terms.
- Apply dictionary replacements after raw transcription.
- Save transcription to DB unless user disables auto-save.

# --- Frontend UI Rules -------------------------------
- All components must use ShadCN UI when applicable.
- Use Tailwind CSS for styling.
- No inline CSS except dynamic styles.
- Prefer server components where possible.
- Client components only when:
  - Audio recording
  - Form interactions
  - Real-time UI updates

- Component Naming:
  - AudioRecorder.tsx
  - TranscriptionCard.tsx
  - DictionaryList.tsx
  - DictionaryForm.tsx

- Never put heavy logic inside client components.
  Use helper functions from /lib.

# --- Validation Rules -------------------------------
- All incoming data must be validated using Zod.
- Create schema files in /lib/validations/.
- For forms: integrate React Hook Form + ZodResolver
- All API inputs must be parsed:
  const parsed = Schema.safeParse(await req.json());

# --- Error Handling -------------------------------
- Must always use try/catch in API routes.
- Return structured errors:
  {
    success: false,
    error: { message: "Invalid input", code: 400 }
  }
- Do not expose internal errors.
- Log server-side errors using console.error (or a logger).

# --- Naming Conventions -----------------------------
- Files: kebab-case (transcription-card.tsx)
- Components: PascalCase
- Hooks: useCamelCase
- API routes: lowercase folders
- Environment variables: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE

# --- Environment Variables --------------------------
Required:
  DATABASE_URL
  NEXTAUTH_SECRET
  NEXTAUTH_URL
  OPENAI_API_KEY or DEEPGRAM_API_KEY
  STORAGE_BUCKET (if used)

- Validate required environment variables at startup.
- Do not commit .env files.

# --- Cursor Branching Rules -------------------------
- For major features, create new branches:
  - feature/transcription
  - feature/dictionary
  - feature/auth
  - fix/api-error-handling

- Cursor should propose optimizations but never modify unrelated files.

# --- Testing Rules ----------------------------------
- Use Jest/Vitest for unit tests.
- Use Playwright for E2E tests.
- API tests must simulate:
  - Authenticated user
  - Invalid payload
  - File upload
  - Dictionary application

# --- Performance Rules -------------------------------
- Never load large audio files into memory unnecessarily.
- Use streaming upload when possible.
- Use pagination for transcription history.
- Avoid N+1 queries by using Prisma includes/selects.

# --- Deployment Rules -------------------------------
- Build must succeed without manual intervention.
- Use Railway or Vercel environment variables.
- Prisma generate must run before build.

# --- Code Quality Rules ------------------------------
- Run ESLint + Prettier before committing.
- No unused variables.
- No console.logs in production code (except error logs).
- Keep components <200 lines where possible.
- Extract repeating code into /lib/utils.ts.

# --- Additional Cursor Behavior ----------------------
- When asked to generate code, Cursor must:
  1. Follow this architecture.
  2. Use strong TypeScript types.
  3. Include Zod validation.
  4. Produce production-grade, clean code.
  5. Add comments explaining complex logic.
  6. Never create global mutable state.

# --- End of Rules -----------------------------------
